<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voline</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Vsevolod Eikhenbaum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Voline, born Vsevolod Mikhailovich Eikhenbaum, was a Russian anarchist writer and activist. After taking part in the 1905 revolution and years of exile, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1917 and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>'s insurgent movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, where he led its cultural and educational work. Expelled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in 1921, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and wrote The Unknown Revolution, an anarchist history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voline.jpg" alt="Voline" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voline_Photographic_Portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vsevolod Eikhenbaum was born on 23 August 1882 into an educated Russian Jewish family from Voronezh. His parents were both doctors, and he and his brother Boris were taught French and German as well as Russian by foreign tutors. He studied law at Saint Petersburg State University, but in 1904 he left to work full time for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionary Party</a>, tutoring workers and setting up a library and study groups. In the 1905 revolution he witnessed Bloody Sunday, took part in founding the Saint Petersburg Soviet and was arrested for a Kronstadt uprising. Arrested again in 1907 and deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, he escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where he met the anarchists Sébastien Faure and Apollon Karelin and by 1911 had become an anarchist himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1915, joined the Union of Russian Workers and wrote for its paper Golos truda, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in July 1917 to promote anarcho-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syndicalism">syndicalism</a>, calling for workers' control. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> he criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, warning of their authoritarian tendencies, and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, where he helped found the Nabat Confederation of Anarchist Organisations. On 18 November 1918 he drew up its declaration of principles, written to suit anarchist communists, individualists and syndicalists alike. In 1919 the Nabat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a> and became a central part of the Makhnovshchina, the movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>.</p><p>There Volin joined its Cultural-Educational Commission, edited its publications and chaired its Military Revolutionary Council. On 14 January 1920 he was arrested while ill with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> and handed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>, with orders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> to execute him. Appeals by anarchists and by the Bolshevik Victor Serge led to his death sentence being commuted, and he was freed in October 1920 under the Starobilsk agreement, refusing an offer of the post of People's Commissar for Education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. He was imprisoned again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, took part in a hunger strike in July 1921 and was then deported from Soviet Russia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he contributed to Sébastien Faure's Anarchist Encyclopedia and to anarchist papers in several languages, and in 1927 criticised the Platform as contradicting decentralisation. He completed The Unknown Revolution in Marseille in 1940 and went into hiding after the Nazi invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the establishment of the collaborationist French State because of his Jewish heritage and his politics. He died of tuberculosis in a Paris hospital on 18 September 1945, and his ashes were buried at the Père Lachaise Cemetery near those of Makhno. Victor Serge called him a man of absolute probity and exceptional rigour of thought.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The movement rejected any state, organising regional congresses of peasants, workers and insurgents, free soviets and communes, while anarchist thinkers such as <b>Volin</b> ran its cultural work.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-territory">Free Territory</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Voronezh, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.67,39.21&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Voronezh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Voline"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5486 5676h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5486 5676h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.67,39.21&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
